CSC 110 Final Project — Prototype I

1. Homework Design: Nested Loops
Project Description
This homework requires students to write a program to set up a system that can be used to separate students into groups.
Students will learn and practice how to use nested loops and conditionals.
This expected system involves 4 tasks:

【1】Ask users to enter 20 student names and check whether the number of students is correct.

【2】Randomly divide the students into five groups of four for the first round.

【3】Ask users whether they want to continue or end the program.

【4】Generate up to five rounds of grouping without repeating any pair of students.




Learning Outcomes
By completing this homework, students will be able to:


【1】Use nested loops to process nested lists.

【2】Understand how inner and outer loops work together.

【3】Use indices to access elements in a two-dimensional list.

【4】Use the modulo operator to calculate indices.

【5】Understand when and how to use while loops and conditionals.

【6】Practice using functions.

【7】Understand how to organize functions and use the main function.




How to Run the Code

【1】Open Homework.py in Thonny.

【2】Click the Run button.

【3】Enter 20 student names separated by commas and space.

【4】The program will display the first round of grouping.

【5】Enter y to generate the next round or n to end the program.

【6】The program allows a maximum of five rounds.





2. In-Class Activity Design: Conditions

Activity Description

This activity asks students to calculate movie ticket prices based on different discount conditions. 
Students are required to discuss and write a program that can decide how much money people should pay 
after asking them to enter their age, height, and whether they qualify for a disability discount 
("Do you qualify for a disability discount? (y/n):").
In the process of doing this activity, students will learn and practice how to set the order of conditional statements 
and how to write specific conditions. This activity also involves 2 small tasks to help students address their misconceptions. 
For task 1, students are given some code examples including different use of == and  =, 
and they are required to determine whether the use is correct and correct it if it is misused. 
For task 2, students are given a special example of someone who satisfied 2 conditions. 
They are asked to think about the order and priority of conditions, 
explaining why only one branch can execute and why the order of conditions is important in deciding the ticket price.



Mistakes and Misconceptions
This activity addresses the following common mistakes:

【1】Confusing if and elif.

【2】Not understanding the order of conditions.

【3】Confusing the assignment operator (=) and the equality operator (==).

【4】Using incorrect comparison operators.

【5】Confusing and and or.

【6】Not considering situations where multiple conditions are true.





How to Run the Code

【1】Open In-Class Activity. py in Thonny.

【2】Click the Run button.

【3】Enter the regular ticket price, age, height, and disability discount eligibility.

【4】The program will calculate and display the final ticket price.




Activity Materials
The activity materials include:

【1】Student exercise and prompts.

【2】Example solution.

